ON THE COLLECTION OF EDUCATION STATISTICS IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES: PURPOSE, PRINCIPLES, PROCEDURES AND PROBLEMS
BIRGER FREDRIKSEN
Division of Economics, Norwegian Institute of International Affairs, Oslo, Norway
1. INTRODUCTION
As a consequence of a vigorous policy in favour of education, most developing countries could, at the threshold of the 198Os, look back on two decades characterized by a growth in pupil numbers unparalleled in history. From being a comparatively small sector, concerning chiefly the lives of a relatively small urban middle and upper class, education has in the span of one generation developed into a large-scale operation, affecting the conditions of life of most sections of the population. This unprecedented expansion of educational systems has placed very heavy demands on the administrators and planners of the education sector. Despite efforts to cope with these demands, the development of national administrative, planning and research capacities often lags behind the growth in size and complexity of the education enterprise. A prerequisite for improving developing countries’ administrative and planning capacities in the field of education is often to improve their capacities to collect and analyze statistical data related to the activities of this sector. The lack of information on key aspects of educational development and the often poor quality of available information have become growing concerns of both producers and users of education statistics. There are many reasons for the present increased realization and appreciation of the
This paper has been prepared as a background for the teaching of education statistics at the Development Statistics course organized by the Institute of Development Studies at the University of Sussex, Brighton, 19 April-16 July 1982.
importance of accurate, detailed and timely education statistics. The sheer size and complexity of the education sector is one. Another factor of growing importance is the fact that the education sector since the mid-1970s has found itself in a tightening financial squeeze between rigid budget ceilings and rapidly rising costs. On the budget side, there is reason to believe that the days when an increase in the share of national income devoted to the education sector could be obtained quite easily may be but a memory. Although there is no obvious ceiling for the proportion of public budgets a government can spend on education, in practice each nation has thresholds beyond which the burden of educational expenditures may seriously cripple the supply of other public services. The end to the preferential treatment enjoyed by education during the past two decades is also related to the present waning confidence in education as the prime mover of development and the growing levels of ‘educated’ unemployment in many countries. As regards the cost side, education remains an extremely labour-intensive sector. As the large number of young teachers recruited in the developing countries during the last two decades stay on in the system, emolument will increase since teachers’ pay scales normally include automatic increases based on length of service and provide for grade increases of teachers improving their qualifications. As the squeeze between budget ceilings and costs grows tighter, many countries will also be faced with difficult choices as regards the priority to be given to expansion of enrolment at different levels of education since the unit cost generally increases steeply by increasing levels of education.
