In short, the need for efficient utilization of available resources is becoming more important than ever. Apart from the above-mentioned tightening financial squeeze and the many unsolved education problems in developing nations, wise spending of public resources is a duty in its own right. And a necessary condition for achieving improvements in this area is, for a good number of developing nations, an improved data base. This is not to say that this is a sufficient condition; statistics alone cannot solve any problem. However, properly collected and analyzed statistics can help administrators and planners to use better existing educational resources both with respect to efficiency (i.e. doing things more rightly) and effectivity (i.e. doing the right things). The major role of statistics is precisely to assist decision-makers by providing the background information needed for informed decisions. The capacity of developing countries to collect and analyze education statistics varies considerably. While some countries have welldeveloped statistical systems, others have only recently started to collect education statistics in a systematic manner. Most countries fall between these two extremes, i.e. they collect education statistics from schools on a routine basis but nevertheless experience serious problems, either as regards the quality, detail and timeliness of the data collected or as regards their capacity to analyze these data. The purpose of the present paper is to discuss some general aspects of data gathering in the field of education. The paper starts by highlighting the main purposes of education statistics. Section 3 discusses some basic principles that education statistics should fulfil. Section 4 reviews six approaches used to a varying degree for the collection of statistics in the field of education while Section 5 treats some special problems experienced in the collection of education statistics in many developing countries. The paper is meant as a general introduction to the purposes, principles, procedures and problems of collecting education statistics in developing countries. The questions of what type of statistics to collect and how to go about the collection and analysis of these statistics are not dealt with here.
2. PURPOSE OF EDUCATION STATISTICS
Education statistics are no end in themselves. They are tools which help us to monitor current developments within the education sector as well as to understand how this complex sector works in order to be able to plan for the future. We may, admittedly somewhat arbitrarily, distinquish between the following six interrelated and overlapping functions that education statistics shall serve:
(i) The descriptive function
The statistics shall describe the current education situation in terms of purpose, pupils, teachers, administrative and other personnel, physical accommodation, material and equipment available as well as the end product in terms of school leavers, graduates and research.
(ii) The comparative function
The statistics shall permit comparisons of educational provision and performance between different institutions, regions, population groups, etc. within a country as well as with educational systems in other countries. Both the value and the many pitfalls of such comparisons should be stressed.
(iii) The monitoring and accounting function
The education sector is a large-scale operation that may account for as much as 20-30070 of the total public budget. It produces the nation’s qualified manpower and recruits in turn a large part of this manpower as students, teachers, administrators, etc. Education is furthermore a highly decentralized activity with a large local and voluntary element. The need for day-to-day administration, monitoring and control of such a complex sector is obvious. To perform these functions well requires a substantial amount of detailed statistics.
(iv) The planning function
Educational planning provides a bridge between the analysis of the current situation and the formulation of policies and programmes for the future. If educational planning is to serve policy-making processes effectively by providing information on likely results of alternative choices, planners must have at their disposal a comprehensive set of reliable and timely statistics.


