(v) The evaluating function
A variety of statistics is required for assessing the performance of the educational system against stated objectives. Fundamentally, there are two types of questions that one is concerned with in making evaluations. The first is whether or not a particular educational programme or action was implemented according to its stated guidelines (‘process evaluation’), the second is the extent to which the programme or action made a change in the desired direction (‘impact evaluation’). Both types of evaluation, carried out independently or together, are essential. Unfortunately, very little evaluation of any type is carried out in the education sector due, partly, to lack of statistical data.
(vi) Educational research or the theorygenerating funclion
This function is naturally closely related to the planning and evaluating functions. Based on accurate and adequately defined and collected education statistics, causal relationships between factors inside as well as outside the educational system may be tested. There is a very urgent need for such research in developing countries, both to study the relationship between educational inputs and outputs (e.g. the effect of teachers, material and equipment on pupils’ performance, attitudes and aspirations) and to study the relationship between education and the society at large (e.g. the effect of the methods of financing on the distribution of educational benefits, the contribution of education to changes in people’s attitude to development, effects of education on the income distribution). Traditionally, the stress in data gathering has been on the descriptive, comparative and monitoring functions rather.than on the planning, evaluating and research functions. This is a direct consequence of the fact that statistical systems in developing countries have evolved largely as by-products of administration, often derived from systems set up by former colonial powers. The importance of educational planning, evaluation and research is however well recognized in developing countries and the urgency of establishing a data base of sufficient detail, accuracy and timeliness to serve these purposes is becoming increasingly clear. However, to establish such a data base requires
the creation of a well-organized data-gathering machinery. This takes time and there is still a long way to go in most developing nations before this will be achieved. The present situation as regards education statistics in developing nations may be illustrated with a quotation from a survey on educational planning in Asia conducted in 1975 by the UNESCO Regional Office for Education in Asia and Oceania. In commenting on the status of ‘research, data and information’ the survey states that:
Despite two decades of educational planning, the educational planners have still to contend with inadequacies in data gathering, storing and retrieving mechanisms. The adequacy, accuracy and quality of data are generally referred to [by countries in the Asian region] as major constraints. The data requirements for planning are now of a different order because of the increasing sophistication of planning techniques. but there has been no corresponding improvement in the data collection machinery. Thus the data collected prove, more often than not, inadequate and even irrelevant. (UNESCO, 1975. P. vi)
The paucity of basic data is considered a serious obstacle to a planned development of education by national planners. For example, in the case of Bangladesh, Qadir and Ahmed note that:
Lack of accurate information on various aspects of primary education was considered since long a major hindrance lo planning education to meet the country’s need. In the absent of sufficient research and educational planning, together with lack of reliable and adequate data, the policies and programmes in the past had lo be based on preconceived notions which had little or no relevance to the social and economic realities of the country. (Quadir and Ahmed, 1980, p. I)
Similar observations are made by a senior educational planner in Thailand commenting upon problems of data availability in his country:
Despite the fact that Thailand devotes more attention to educational statistics than many developing countries, the planners - both at the central level and in the Provinces and Regions - are still confronted with substantial statistical problems. (Tajaroensuk, 1975, p. 135)
Similar observations are made for African
