3. PRINCIPLES FOR COLLECTION OF EDUCATION STATISTICS
The practical problems encountered when employing the education statistics available in developing countries for the purpose of planning and administration are many, and they vary between countries, types of statistic and
uses to which the statistics are put. Notwithstanding such differences, some problems are common to most countries, types of statistic and uses. For example, data are often incomplete, inaccurate or both. They are furthermore often out of date and too aggregated. Some types of statistics essential for planning purposes (e.g. data on pupil and teacher flows, cost data) are often missing, and so on. We shall list below some general principles that should guide the decision-maker as regards which data to collect and how to collect them. It will be clear from studying this list that the principles included are not mutually exclusive. Furthermore, they compete with each other. No set of data can be perfect, one has always to weigh improvements in one dimension against sacrifices in another. For example, to increase the number of elements on which data are collected may, for the same level of resources, reduce the accuracy of the data collected and/or increase the time-lag between collection and publication. Gains in accuracy may imply increases in costs as would most other improvements required in the data presently available in developing countries. The tradeoffs between the various principles listed must therefore be decided for each country taking due account of national conditions and constraints. (The order in which the items are included in the following list appear does not indicate order of importance.)
(i) Data should be based on unambiguous definitions
Although this might at first sight appear to be a requirement too obvious to deserve inclusion in this list, it is unfortunately true that this basic principle of data gathering often is not sufficiently observed. It is therefore necessary to stress at the outset the overriding importance of developing clear and unambiguous definitions for all elements on which information is to be collected. The pitfalls are many in this area. Terms which at first appear to be quite clearly defined may on reflection be rather vague. Suffice it to say here that there sometimes is doubt about the meaning of even basic terms such as enrolment, repeaters, promotees and uastage. It is useful in this connection to distinguish between two aspects, i.e. cases where the definition of a


