given term varies between countries and cases where different suppliers and/or users of data within a given country interpret national definitions differently. As regards the first aspect, we should like to stress the importance of a standardized statistical terminology for the purpose of international comparisons in the field of education. Although the planning and administration of education are very largely purely national concerns, the problems encountered in this field are often similar in nature in many countries, and much can therefore be learned from studying other countries’ experiences and politics. However for such international comparisons of educational developments and policies to be worthwhile they must be based on common terms of reference. An essential aspect of such common terms of reference is that any statistics that are used must have, as nearly as possible, the same meaning in the different countries involved. Even when data are used for purely national purposes, to employ the terms proposed in international recommendations concerning educational statistics will in most cases present definite advantages. For example, many of the methods of educational planning most commonly employed are based on the use of educational statistics collected according to these definitions. This is for instance the case for the simple pupil flow models used for reconstructing cohort flows and for projecting school enrolment. These models imply a particular way of defining new entrants, repeaters, promotees and dropouts. In a case where these flows are defined otherwise, this may imply that the results drawn from such models cannot be interpreted in the usual way. As an example, we mention that some countries define ‘repeaters’ as ‘pupils who fail the examination at the end of a given school year’ (i.e. ‘the failures’) instead of as ‘pupils who at the beginning of two consecutive school-years are enrolled in the same grade’. If this is the case, the meaning of the results calculated for cohort flow indicators of the type normally derived for pupil flow models (e.g. survival by grade, graduation and wastage) will not be as described in textbooks presenting such models. With respect to the second aspect mentioned above, i.e. different suppliers and/or users of
data within a given country interpreting national definitions differently, this happens quite frequently. In particular, those who are the primary suppliers of education statistics, i.e. educational institutions, may not always have the time, resources and trained personnel required to give the completion of the annual questionnaires the necessary attention. The questionnaire itself may moreover not be sufficiently clear, the definitions of terms may not be precise enough, and so on.
(ii) Data should be complete
There are at least two ways in which the educational statistics available in developing countries is often incomplete. Firstly, the coverage may be incomplete in the sense that not all pupils, teachers, etc. are included. One common cause of incomplete data is that some schools are not included on the list of schools to which questionnaires are sent. Incomplete school-lists is a problem common to many developing countries. There are many reasons for schools not being included on the school lists. Public education is often provided by many ministries as well as by a wide array of government agencies, and all of these activities may not be known to those responsible for data collection. Furthermore, privately managed education plays a significant role in most developing nations. There is also the problem of unrecognized schools, i.e. institutions which, for various reasons, are not recognized by the Ministry of Education’. Another serious concern in many developing countries is the fact that some schools, although included on the school list, do not complete the questionnaires. The second way in which data are often incomplete in developing nations is the lack of certain key data elements absolutely essential to carrying out even the most rudimentary planning of educational activities. As an example of such data we mention statistics on pupil flows in countries where the level of repetition and/or dropout is significant, and the very common paucity of data on education costs. We shall return to this problem under point (vi) below. Lack of key data and incomplete coverage of existing data are serious obstacles to efficient planning and monitoring of educational systems in developing nations. For example,


296 B. FREDRIKSEN
how can a country’s progress towards targets such as universal primary education be assessed if, due to incomplete coverage of data, we do not know how many children are attending school? How can future school enrolment, teacher requirements and costs be projected if one does not know how many of the children of school-going age have already entered school? As an example of the problem lack of key data poses for educational planners, we quote the following statement made by a national planner concerning the situation in Thailand in the mid-seventies. In commenting on ‘unavailability of important data’ the report states:
The major statistical problem faced by planners during the preparation of the Third Five-Year Plan (covering the period 1971-1976) was the unavailability of large amounts of essential data directly connected with educational systems as well as other data needed for planning. As far as data directly connected with education are concerned there was almost a complete lack of any data in the following fields: (I) institutional unit costs; (2) sources of finance; (3) availability of physical facilities; (4) utilization of physical facilities; (5) age and standard of existing facilities; (6) wastage through student dropout and repetition; (7) field of specialization of teachers; (8) proportion of newly qualified teacher trainees becoming teachers. (Tajaroensuk, 1975, p. 136)
The report goes on to discuss how the lack of these key data items complicates the planning process. The data items included in this quotation cover the aspects on which statistics are most generally lacking in developing countries. Finally, we note that the problem of incomplete data is further complicated when there are yearly variations in the coverage of the data which are available due, for example to fluctuations in the response rate. In the case of data on enrolment, this may cause considerable variations in the flow rates.
(iii) Data should be accurate
Although this is a rather obvious basic requirement, the issue of accuracy is not as clearcut as it might seem. Few, if any, sets of education statistics need to be one hundred percent accurate; indeed, it would in most cases be an unwise use of scarce resources to aim at such a high level of precision. The level of accuracy needed for planning purposes moreover differs between different types of information. For example, while it would be essential to
achieve a high level of accuracy for the data collected on the number of pupils and teachers, a lower level of precision might be acceptable for statistics pertaining to certain pupil and teacher characteristics (e.g. age, socioeconomic background, causes of dropout). Every effort should be made to achieve a high level of accuracy for the minimum set of data collected through the annual school census. For other types of data needed on an ad hoc basis to study specific educational issues, a lower level of precision might sometimes be acceptable. For the sake of greater speed and economy, it is often possible in such cases to employ estimates based on secondary sources rather than on direct measurement. After having established an adequate machinery for conducting the annual school censuses, the first step towards ensuring accuracy is to identify and guard against possible biases in reporting. For example, if grants or teacher assignments are based upon enrolment, this may be an incentive for schools to inflate their enrolment figures. Attendance may likewise be exaggerated because low attendance rates may reflect badly upon teachers and administrators. Apart from more or less deliberate misreporting of this type, a large number of sources of errors exist. The problem of ambiguous definitions of terms has already been mentioned above. Other types of error result from inaccuracy in recording of the information, from adding up the questionnaires as well as from copying the statistics from one form to another. The importance of scrutiny and correction of errors at all stages in the collection and compilation of statistics cannot be overemphasized. All too often the usefulness of data which are collected at great cost is severely impaired by carelessness especially in copying and checking.
(iv) Data should be sufficiently disaggregated At the outset data are collected at the school level and are later on aggregated. Aggregation means loss of information. Frequently, the only data readily accessible to planners, research and policy-makers refer to the nation as a whole or, at best, to geographical or administrative regions. For many types of studies such data are too aggregated, e.g. studies of the impact of school input variables (i.e. teachers, instruc


