tional material, class size, etc.) on the pupils’ performance. Ideally it should be possible to relate one variable to another at least at the school level. This enhances greatly the utility of the statistics. Such flexibility is however not obtained free of cost, and it requires that data be gathered and compiled in such a way that they can be used for a number of different purposes.
(v) Data should be available on a timely basis A problem common to many developing countries is a quite long time period between the date at which education statistics are collected and the date at which they are made available to the users. Illustrations of this rather sad state of affairs can be found in education development plans of many countries where, in order to have an enrolment figure for the year the plan was established, one has often had to project two or three years ahead of the latest year for which data were available. This means that in order to prepare an enrolment projection to be included in, say, a five-year plan one actually needs to project six or seven years ahead of the latest observed figure. For example, in the case of Thailand, Tajaroensuk states that:
At the time when the detailed projections for the Third Five-Year Plan were made (during 1970). the latest complete school census available was that for 1967. Thus the projection for the period 1971-1976 sometimes had to
be based on data no more recent than 1967 . . (Tajaroensuk, 1975, p. 136)
It is obvious that this increases considerably (and unnecessarily) the uncertainty of the projection. Lack of up-to-date statistics is a serious concern to planners and policy-makers. How can one monitor current education trends if it takes years to know what these trends are? In general, a long time period between collection and publication greatly reduces the utility of the data for administrative and planning purposes. In the worst case data made available long after the period for which they are gathered may become obsolete before they ever reach the planner or decision-maker. This would mean that the considerable amount of scarce human and financial resources spent on collecting, compiling and publishing the data are to a large extent wasted.
To speed up the process will in general mean actions at the school level to encourage quicker response to the questionnaires, more trained clerical staff at the regional and central level to verify, rectify, compile and consolidate the data and more equipment in terms of calculators, duplicators, etc. The point here is that this would generally be a quite small investment compared to the resources already used directly and indirectly in collecting the data. One remedy often proposed in order to reduce the time-lag between collection and publication is to use computers for the processing of the data once they reach the education statistics unit. We shall discuss this point briefly in Section 5. Suffice it to say here that, although the use of computers presents several advantages once the data are ready for processing, most of the delay in making education statistics available to the users is not due to long processing time, but to the time it takes to collect, verify and rectify the data. Timeliness is clearly in competition with some of the criteria already mentioned above, e.g. completeness, degree of detail and, to some extent, accuracy. It is often preferable for the planners and policy-makers to have data which are somewhat imperfect in terms of completeness, detail and accuracy when they need them rather than better data when it is too late.
(vi) Data should be user-related
A quite common experience among users of statistical information is that data tend to be compiled in a way that prevents meaningful analyses of issues which require the combination of several sets of data. An important example is budget and expenditure data which are usually categorized by item (e.g. teacher salaries, textbooks, chalk) rather than by function (teaching, administration, research) or by type and level of education. This makes it very difficult, if not impossible, to relate these statistics to other sets of data to study aspects such as various types of unit cost (e.g. teaching cost per pupil, administration cost per pupil or school, average pupil cost at different levels of education). It is even difficult to calculate basic measures such as pupil-teacher ratios by level of education in countries where teachers are engaged in teaching at, e.g. both first and second level education.


