The above problem may be illustrated by a quotation from an Indian study. In a section commenting on the difficulty of linking expenditure data to other types of data the report states:
It is also difficult to relate expenditure to enrolments. While enrolments are given bv stages and schools, expenditure is reported by institut-ions. An institution is designated by the highest class it teaches; many institutions have more than one stage. A high school may have primary, middle and high school classes; it is, therefore, difficult to determine precisely the per student cost of education at various levels of education. (Saraf el al., 1980, p. 180)
In order to organize the data in a form that maximizes their potential utility for the users, it is necessary for those responsible for gathering the statistics to have a fairly clear idea of what uses these statistics might be put to. As explained in Section 2 time and effort spent on clarifying these questions at an early state in the datagathering process make it possible to avoid collecting (at great cost) information which later on proves to be ill-suited to analytical purposes. To ensure that the data collected are userrelated is a two-way process in which administrators as well as planners and statisticians need to cooperate closely.
(vii) Data should be accessible
All too often data collected at great cost are practically inaccessible to many user groups. In the worst case (and such cases exist) the information on pupils, teachers, etc., is not even extracted from the questionnaire returns received from the schools. A more common situation is that the information is compiled and summary tabulations are prepared and used for planning purposes within the Ministry of Education. However, these data are not necessarily published in a form that makes them available to a wide audience. Even when a publication is prepared, it sometimes takes a very long time to appear and is issued in a rather limited number of copies. Lack of resources and low priority for education statistics publications within the government printshop are two common reasons for such delays. For example, in the case of India, Saraf et al. say that:
The data having been processed, is prepared in manuscript form for printing and is sent to the Government of India Press. The press usually takes more than a
year to supply printed copies of the publication as it has to cater to the needs of all the Central klinistries, Parliament and other Government organizations. (Saraf el al., 1950, p. 177)
This completes our brief review of general principles for collection of education statistics. In the following section we shall mention some principles for data-gathering procedures.
