Regular censuses of all educational institutions; Regular sample surveys covering selected data items; Ad hoc sample surveys to provide information on topics of special interest; Population censuses; Sample surveys drawn from the total population, usually in conjunction with population censuses; Routine reporting of data obtained as a by-product of educational administration. We shall below comment on each of these six ways of obtaining education statistics. Two points should, however, be stressed at the outset. Firstly, not all of the above six ways of data collection are equally relevant for all types of education statistics. For example, while ad hoc sample surveys are very useful for collecting data needed for special studies (e.g. on factors such as causes of drop-out and repetition, reasons for pupils not attending school regularly, socioeconomic background of pupils, work aspiration of school leavers), data on other items (e.g. on teachers, pupils, finance) need to be collected on a regular basis through censuses covering all institutions. Secondly, whichever of the six methods is used in obtaining the data, there are two basic approaches to the collection and processing of the statistics. The first is based on aggregated statistics on data items such as pupils, teachers, various types of educational facilities, finance,


