etc., supplied by the basic reporting unit, usually the educational institution. The statistics are aggregated in the sense that they cover the fofal number of pupils enrolled in each grade, the total number of teachers in the school, etc. The second approach is individual reporting where institutions, instead of submitting aggregated data for the entire institution, submit information on each component of the aggregate. This implies that each individual covered is given a unique code number on which all information referring to this individual is recorded. The reporting can be the result of answers to questionnaires filled out by the individual himself or by the responsible authority (e.g. the school). This form of data collection is generally referred to as an individualized data system (ID system). The main distinction between such a system and a system which is not based on individualized data is that in the case of an ID system the permanent individual characteristics of pupils and teachers (e.g. sex, year of birth, background data) would need to be recorded only once (i.e. when the pupil or teacher first entered the institution) and all matching of current educational activity with previous experience and with individual characteristics can be done based on this recording. In the case of a system not based on individual data all the data required on individual characteristics of pupils and teachers must be especially recorded each time it is required. Among the main advantages of data collected through an ID system as compared to aggregate data is the elimination of double counting of teachers and pupils. The system is particularly well suited to the collection of flow statistics on these two main data elements. Accurate and detailed flow statistics can be derived easily, and the storage of the statistics is flexible and permits quick production of crosstabulations. It is, however, clear that to derive maximum advantage from such a system requires the use of computers. Notwithstanding the above advantages, only a very limited number of industrialized countries have so far introduced an ID system for education statistics. No developing country has installed such a system. There are many reasons for choosing to rely on the more traditional system of aggregated statistics. In addition to
the very high initial costs of establishing an ID system, developing countries often lack the equipment and trained manpower essential for operating such a system. Furthermore, to be certain that the investment is worthwhile, a data collection procedure must be established which is certain to produce reasonably reliable base data. No useful purpose is served by installing a costly system for storing unreliable data. There is also in many countries considerable resistance to the idea of a centralized individual register on pupils. Moreover, decentralized statistics are required in a decentralized educational system and special care would have to be taken to ensure an efficient and rapid feedback of information from the central register to the various levels of local administration as well as to the individual institutions. Thus, the use of an ID system for collection of education statistics in developing countries is very much a question for the future. This aspect will, therefore, not be further discussed in this paper.
(i) Regular censuses of educational institutions. Regular censuses of educational institutions form the backbone of the education data collection system of practically every country. To carry out such a census normally implies that the person responsible for administering each institution (e.g. the headmaster or headteacher of each school) answers a number of questions concerning his institution. For the most part these questions would deal with the pupils and teachers in the institution, but they can also concern equipment, finance, school buildings and other facilities. Usually the information is collected through questionnaires. The carrying out of questionnaire surveys for the purpose of collecting education statistics gives rise to many questions which cannot be discussed here. Unlike population censuses which normally are carried out once every tenth year and which cover the whole population enumerated at one particular date, censuses of educational institutions are normally carried out annually, their coverage is limited to the educational system and different data elements may be enumerated at different points in time (e.g. pupils at the beginning of the year and the expenditures at the end). The date at which the census is held is


