an important aspect since the number of pupils and teachers normally changes during the school year. It is generally recommended that the data on pupils and teachers should refer to the beginning of the school year. By ‘beginning’ is normally meant a month or so after the first school day of the school year. This is particularly important for the enumeration of pupils in countries where school attendance is not strictly compulsory and where some of the children who turn up the first days of the school year may, for various reasons, quit school after a short period. If the count refers to a date after the school year has started, these early dropouts may be omitted from the register. Inclusion in school registers of pupils who do not attend school at all (apart from perhaps a few days) occurs in many developing countries. Such errors are significant in many countries. They lead to overestimation of enrolment and may cause considerable distortion in the estimate of the promotion, repetition and dropout rates. A related phenomenon common to many developing countries is high dropout during the school year, i.e. pupils who do attend several months before leaving. Such intrayear dropout is particularly found in the first grade of primary education. In cases where intrayear dropout does take place, it implies that the enrolment figures reported by the school are affected by the date chosen for the count as well as by the procedure followed for exluding from the register pupils who do not attend school regularly. In order to cope with this problem many countries (particularly in Latin America) count their pupils more than once during the school year. As already mentioned, the regular census of educational institutions forms the backbone of the education data collection system in all countries. Although the other five ways of collecting data mentioned above may provide useful, and in some cases, indispensable supplementary information, these other methods can in no way replace the annual school census. (ii) and (iii) Regular and ad hoc sample
