total population. For a random sample it is theoretically possible, by making certain assumptions, to calculate the size of the sampling error associated uith a given sample size as well as the probability of the occurence of the error. This means that the estimate of the sampling error can be used to determine the size of the sample required to keep this error within the limits found acceptable to those performing the investigation. Survey error: This refers to errors such as incorrect counting by the investigator, incorrect responses by the persons interviewed, errors in recording of the responses, errors in the transcription, computation and compilation of the data, etc. This is often an important source of error, particularly in developing nations.
Time-sampling error: This type of error
arises from the uneven occurrence of events over time. For example, in the field of education, the results derived from a sample investigation to determine enrolment will, due to intrayear dropout and irregular attendance, depend on the time during the school year at which the investigation is carried out.
Sampling techniques are quite extensively used in developing countries for collecting statistics in fields such as demography, employment, agriculture and health. So far such techniques have however not been much used in the field of education. This is unfortunate since many types of education statistics could be fully or partly collected through sample investigations. For example, commenting upon the costeffectiveness of various data-gathering techniques, McMeekin says that:
One means of reducing data-gathering costs is through sample studies. The reliability of sample studies (given certain conditions) is not generally understood by non statisticians. Few administrators of developing educational systems recognize that a well-designed sample survey can produce policy information that is virtually as accurate as a one-hundred percent census; and that sampling can improve the manageability of data by avoiding data glut, reducing costs, and increasing speed of analysis. The net result is that it is often possible IO obtain useful policy information more effectively through sampling
than through full surveys . . (hlchleekin, 1975, p. 64)


