For example, sample surveys could be used to: (a) check the accuracy of data collected through the annual school census; (b) collect statistics on elements on which information is needed for ad hoc studies and for formulating policy on particular topics; (c) collect statistics on items on which data are not needed annually and which, consequently, should not form part of the annual school questionnaire; (d) collect detailed background information of a type that can best be obtained by the use of trained investigators rather than through mail questionnaires. The use of sampling techniques in the field of education should be regarded as a way of supplementing the information collected through the annual school census, which will remain the backbone of the education data-gathering system. However, to benefit from this approach developing countries need to train statisticians and investigators in sample survey techniques and procedures. The UNESCO Office of Statistics is concurrently promoting
studies in this area.
