information on the current situation in the school system is collected mainly from educational institutions, information on the educational characteristics of the population at large is obtained from general population censuses and surveys associated with such censuses. The population census questions related to education generally cover literacy, educational attainment and school attendance. As we in this report are mainly concerned with current educational statistics, collected directly from institutions through annual school census, we shall not discuss further problems related to the collection of data on educational attainment and literacy.
(vi) Data that emerge as a by-product from administrative needs. As stated previously,
statistical systems in developing countries have evolved largely as a product of educational administration. Administrative records constitute the main source of information on important aspects such as educational finance and expenditures, teacher salaries and qualifications, scholarships, examination
results, etc. The need for administrative purposes of the above types of statistics is obvious. Education is a large-scale operation accounting for as much as 20-30’7’0 of public budgets in developing countries. Detailed financial statistics are therefore required for budgeting and accounting purposes. Moreover, to monitor educational standards as well as the output of the educational system in terms of school-leavers and graduates requires data on examination results. The Ministry of Education is moreover one of the largest employers in any country and quite detailed personnel records must necessarily be kept for administrative purposes such as control of the payment, of enrolments, for fixing salary scales, for promotion, appointment policies, etc. Faced with this situation whereby a large part of the statistics needed for planning, evaluation and research purposes are contained in files and registers designed solely for administrative purposes, the task of the educational statisticians and planners is not to attempt to control these statistics, but to ensure they are designed and stored in such a way that they can also serve non-administrative purposes. In closing this section we would like to stress that in choosing between the various possible procedures of data gathering discussed above, the question of costs is crucial. Just as costeffectiveness techniques should be applied to education programmes, they should similarly guide the selection between alternative ways of collecting a given set of statistics. If proper cost-benefit studies were performed one would probably find that a significant part of the data presently collected through the school censuses could have been collected both more accurately and at lower costs through sample surveys.
5. PROBLEMS FACED BY DEVELOPING COUNTRIES IN IMPROVING THEIR DATA BASE
Section 3 discussed some general principles that education statistics should meet. As pointed out in that section, many developng countries are not always able to adhere strictly to these principles and their data base consequently suffers from a number of shortcom


302 B. FREDRIKSEN
ings. We shall in the present section highlight some special problems faced by those in charge of education statistics in developing countries.
(i) Lack of trained staff at all levels
The reason most commonly given to explain shortcomings in the statistics available on education in developing countries is lack of trained staff. There is a severe shortage of qualified statisticians working in this field. Equally important is the shortage of assistants and clerks who have undergone at least a brief training programme in basic statistical notions and techniques. For example, summarizing his experience from visiting nine African countries the erstwhile Regional Adviser on Education Statistics in Africa states that:
In most of the countries visited the unit responsible for educational statistics has a very limited and insufficienly trained staff. In the extreme case, only one official is working in the \rhole area of data collection. compilation, analysis and publication of school statistics. (UNESCO, 1977. p. 8)
The lack of trained staff is felt at all levels of data gathering. It is in fact important to keep in mind that, with the exception of countries with comparatively small populations, the questionnaires submitted by schools pass through one or more local/provincial/regional/state administration(s) before reaching the unit responsible for education statistics at the national level*. The work of these local administrations is often decisive in determining the quality in terms of accuracy, completeness and timeliness of the education statistics collected. It is in many cases no exaggeration to say that while the extent to which the data are actually used for planning purposes largely depends on the competence of the staff at the central level, the quality of the data largely depends on the availability of trained inspectors, planners and administrators at the local level. This is so because many of the errors commonly found in education statistics are committed at the source (e.g. reporting errors made by the schools) or during the compilation and consolidation of the questionnaires at the local and provincial levels. These kinds of mistake are difficult both to trace and correct at the central level. Improved staffing at the lower levels of the data collection hierarchy, combined with training of those completing the questionnaires at the school level are, therefore, two
necessary conditions for improving the quality of education statistics in developing countries. As an illustration of the lack of trained staff at the local level in some countries we quote the following statement from a survey of the problems of collecting education statistics carried out by the Pakistani authdrities. Reporting on the situation in Punjab it is said that:
At the district level. there is no such person who is uell trained for the purpose of collection of educational statistics. To talk of a trained statistical hand at district level is an imaginary proposition. at prebent in 311 the district of Punjab there is not even a single person \tho is exclusively meant for the job of collection of true educational statistics (Bureau of Educational Planning and
hlanagemcnt. 1976. p. I)
With respect to Baluchistan the report states that:
No routine [of collecting education statistics] has a\ yet been established, bccaute of inadequacy of staff. (Bureau of Educational Management. 1976. p. 7)
Among the main reasons for this lack of officers trained in the collection and analysis of education statistics, we mention the general shortage of trained statisticians in many developing nations, the low priority given to statistics in general and to education statistics in particular, and the tendency of trained education statisticians to transfer to more prestigious fields of statistics such as economic statistics. We shall return to these aspects below.
(ii) Low priority and prestige of education statistics
Traditionally education statistics, as well as statistics on other social concerns, have been given a much lower priority in developing countries than have various types of economic statistics. This stress on economic statistics is no doubt partly a consequence of the prevailing development strategy of the post-war era. This strategy has been centered around economic growth through industrialization and it has not always paid sufficient attention to various social and distributional aspects of the growth process. This being said, the strategy did give a high priority to the education sector as the main provider of qualified manpower. In view of this fact and considering the large proportion of public budgets spent on education, the scant


COLLECTION OF EDUCATION STATISTICS IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 303
attention paid to producing reliable, detailed and timely statistics on the activities of this sector is quite surprising. One result of the low priority given to education statistics is naturally a correspondingly low score as regards the allocation of resources to the collection and analysis of this type of statistic. This has meant a lack of staff and equipment. Furthermore, it has often led to education statisticians having poorer working conditions in terms of pay and career possibilities than statisticians employed in more prestigious fields of statistics. The education sector has therefore often lost out in the competition for qualified staff with other sectors of the economy. This applies to the initial recruitment of qualified staff as well as to the sector’s ability to retain staff trained on the job. Frequent transfer of qualified personnel to other sectors is a problem in many developng countries.
(iii) Lack of equipment
This aspect has already been mentioned above but it deserves a separate point. Basic equipment such as typewriters, duplicating machines and calculators is very often lacking, particularly at the local level. For example, for India, Saraf et al. say that:
At the district level calculating machines or other mechanical aids are not available. Compilation of data is. therefore, mostly done manually at this level. Manual calculations not only result in delay in the compilation work but also contribute to the errors in calculations. (Saraf er ol., 1980, p. 174)
The use of computers for processing education statistics is often discussed. Such facilities are perhaps not a top priority at present in countries where the quality of the data available is as low as is presently the case in many developing countries. In such countries the returns on the investments which would be required to computerize education statistics would perhaps be higher if these resources were spent on the data-gathering side (e.g. on improving accuracy, detail and completeness of the data) rather than on speeding up the processing of the low quality statistics presently collected. But, naturally, at some point it will be in the interest of most countries to start using elec
tronic computers for storing and processing of education statistics. When this point is reached depends on national conditions and must be decided by each individual country in each case. With respect to equipment, the report of the Regional Adviser on Education Statistics in Africa quoted above states that:
. A radical improvement of educational statistics is called for, but this must primarily be achieved through concerted action to reinforce the responsible unit, both with staff and equipment. A few of the countries have experimented with computer processing of their primary school statistics but not with entirely positive results. Such processing alone does not solve all the problems for these small units and should be introduced only after careful planning of all the phases in data collection and compilation. (UNESCO, 1977, p. 9)
(iv) Lack of co-operation and support
Those responsible for education statistics in developing countries often feel a lack of support and co-operation from schools as well as from other units and agencies within the administration. At the school level there is often a lack of appreciation as regards the importance of the data collected through the annual questionnaires. Furthermore, experience shows that headmasters, often overloaded with administrative and pedagogical responsibilities, cannot always be expected to devote enough time and interest to the filling in of statistical questionnaires. Particular attention needs moreover to be given to the role of school inspectors, local education officers and heads of educational institutions in the process of statistical data collection. Close and trustful collaboration between the various links of the data-gathering chain is a necessary condition for improving the quality of education statistics. Particular problems of co-operation are often experienced with private schools as well as with public agencies providing instruction in institutions not controlled by the Ministry of Education. It is important that all educational institutions be obliged by law to complete the annual school questionnaires.
(v) Problems of communications
Among the many special problems faced by those responsible for education statistics in developing countries we mention, finally, the


304 B. FREDRIKSEN
very poor means of communication in many countries. This complicates the transmission of questionnaires to and from the schools as well as the process of rectifying errors and the collection of supplementary information to fill in gaps. Few primary schools in rural areas have telephones, they may be in remote areas far from roads, etc. The importance of close cooperation between .headmasters, school inspectors and local education officers is, of course, even more important under such conditions than in countries where the central unit can contact schools easily by telephone.
